# Documentation (copied during build, removed in Dockerfile.mcp)
*.md
!package.json

# Test artifacts. `tests/` itself is NOT excluded, deliberately.
#
# `src/core/config/scripts/generateOpsIndex.mjs` (upstream's file, byte-identical) writes two
# indexes: src/core/operations/index.mjs and tests/operations/index.mjs. It readdir's
# tests/operations/tests/ unconditionally, so excluding `tests` from the build context makes
# `npx grunt configTests` die with ENOENT.
#
# That was previously invisible: the Gruntfile chained its config scripts with `;`, so the chain's
# exit status was the trailing `echo` and the failure was swallowed -- the image built "green" with
# the ops index already written and the error discarded. Chaining with `&&` surfaced it.
#
# The image is unaffected either way: Dockerfile.mcp's builder stage `rm -rf`s `tests` before the
# runtime stage copies /app, so this costs 3.7 MB of build context and nothing in the final image.
# Excluding it instead would mean hand-editing an upstream-mirrored file, which the next sync
# reverts.

# Test and coverage OUTPUT, as opposed to the `tests` sources above. These are produced by a local
# `npm run test:coverage` / `npm test` run and are pure build-context weight -- nothing in the
# image reads them, and a stale local copy would be silently baked into the layer.
coverage
.nyc_output
test-results

# The upstream reference checkout. A populated `ref-proj/CyberChef` is a second full copy of the
# source tree; it exists only so the sync can three-way diff against upstream and has no business
# in a build context. Spotted by listing /app in the built image, where it had survived because
# the builder stage's `rm -rf` list never named it.
ref-proj

# IDE and OS files
*.log
